-- Int64: abstract spec for the built-in integer sort.
-- Spec satisfaction facts (Eq[Int64], Ord[Int64], Numeric[Int64]) live in
-- the per-language binding files (e.g. rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/boundedint.anthill)
-- — see proposal 038.
sort anthill.prelude.Int64
  import anthill.prelude.{Eq, Ord, Numeric}
  -- WI-894: `ite` is a RULE-INTRODUCED functor on `Bool`, reached by an explicit
  -- import rather than by a global name — see the note in `ordered.anthill`.
  import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}
  -- WI-20260825-KD9SW: a WRITTEN bare `eq` (an effect guard, a law) names the
  -- operation by import now; a minted `=`/`!=` carries its own address and needs nothing.
  -- WI-882 dropped `neq` from this list: its only written occurrences were the three
  -- `neq(?b, 0)` denial constraints that duplicated the `Error[DivisionByZero]` rows.
  import anthill.prelude.PartialEq.{eq}

  -- WI-876: `Int64`'s ORDERING, declared here and backed by the host in
  -- `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/int64.anthill`'s `operation_map`. Body-less
  -- because the implementation is the host artifact's — the `LogicalStream.
  -- splitFirst` shape. Since WI-880 the mapping is what `check_provider_operations`
  -- reads to accept it: the exemption is per OPERATION, so a body-less member with no
  -- `operation_map` entry is refused even on a host carrier.
  --
  -- WHY DECLARED AT ALL, when `fact Ord[T = Int64]` alone used to do: the host
  -- implementation had nowhere to be KEYED, so it was registered on the SPEC op
  -- (`anthill.prelude.Ord.compare`) — where that one host-scalar implementation
  -- served every carrier that never wrote its own, including structural ones it
  -- cannot compare. An operation must EXIST on the carrier for a per-carrier
  -- registration to attach to; these declarations are that.
  --
  -- `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` are host-backed as well as `compare`, not left to
  -- `PartialOrd`'s `compare`-based default: that keeps the scalar comparison ONE
  -- host call, and it gives the default body a floor to bottom out on (it reads
  -- `Int64.gt` on `compare`'s result).
  operation {
    compare(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]   -- -1 | 0 | 1
    gt(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Bool @[host_implemented]         -- >
    gte(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Bool @[host_implemented]        -- >=
    lt(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Bool @[host_implemented]         -- <
    lte(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Bool @[host_implemented]        -- <=
    max(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]       -- the greater
    min(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]       -- the lesser
  }

  -- Bounds: Int64 is finite (platform-dependent, e.g. i64)
  --
  -- WI-884: host-backed, named in `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/int64.anthill`'s
  -- `operation_map`. Until then these were the only two of this sort's eighteen
  -- declared operations backed by NOTHING — both died `OperationBodyMissing` on a
  -- program that loaded clean, the shape WI-881 found twenty-four times over on
  -- `Float`, and invisible for the same reason (a host carrier was exempt from the
  -- load-time backing check WHOLESALE — WI-880 narrowed it to a per-operation question,
  -- so an unrealized body-less operation is a load error here now).
  --
  -- A HOST MAPPING AND NOT AN EQUATION, though `minValue() <=> ...` would be exact
  -- for a fixed carrier: a `@[simp]` head is an APPLICATION, so it rewrites
  -- `minValue()` and not the BARE `minValue` a call site may write.
  -- MEASURED on `Float.tau`, which is why the three float constants are host-backed
  -- too. Nullary operations want dispatch, not inlining.
  --
  -- WI-882 DELETED `constraint in_bounds: gte(?n, minValue), lte(?n, maxValue)` from
  -- just below these two. It was GUARDLESS — no `:-`, so it quantified over every
  -- Int64 value there is — and it was not the precondition of anything; there is no
  -- assertion and no call site it could be checked at. It was also inert, like every
  -- plain denial (§8.4), so it stated nothing that fired. "Every Int64 is within
  -- bounds" is true by construction of the carrier, not a claim the KB can discharge.
  operation minValue() -> Int64 @[host_implemented]
  operation maxValue() -> Int64 @[host_implemented]

  -- WI-880: `Int64`'s ARITHMETIC, declared here for the same reason the ordering above
  -- is — a host implementation has to have a carrier to be keyed to. `Additive.add` /
  -- `sub` and `Multiplicative.mul` used to carry ONE registration that served every
  -- carrier and tested its operands to find out which arithmetic it was implementing;
  -- `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/int64.anthill`'s `operation_map` now names
  -- `int_add` / `int_sub` / `int_mul`, and those are the CHECKED ones (an overflow
  -- raises rather than wrapping — `Float`'s saturate and `BigInt`'s cannot overflow,
  -- which is three operations that were sharing a name).
  --
  -- DECLARING THEM MOVES NAME RESOLUTION INSIDE THIS SORT, exactly as WI-876's
  -- comparisons did: a bare `add` in a rule here now means `Int64.add`, not
  -- `Additive.add`, so the `import anthill.prelude.Additive.{add, sub}` this sort used
  -- to carry is gone — it would have been shadowed by these declarations, and a
  -- shadowed import is a claim that reads as live.
  operation {
    add(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]        -- +, checked
    sub(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]        -- - (binary), checked
    mul(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]        -- *, checked
  }

  -- Operations beyond what Numeric provides
  operation {
    abs(a: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]                  -- |a|
    neg(a: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]                  -- -a
    mod(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) } @[host_implemented]      -- a mod b (always non-negative); partial: b ≠ 0
    rem(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) } @[host_implemented]      -- a rem b (sign follows dividend); partial: b ≠ 0
    div(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) } @[host_implemented]      -- truncated integer division; also `/` infix; partial: b ≠ 0
    divExact(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) } @[host_implemented] -- integer division (truncated); alias of div; partial: b ≠ 0
    sign(a: Int64) -> Int64 @[host_implemented]                 -- -1, 0, or 1
    to_float(n: Int64) -> Float @[host_implemented]           -- exact for |n| < 2^53; rounds otherwise
    to_string(n: Int64) -> String @[host_implemented]         -- decimal representation, no padding
  }

  -- Laws
  rule neg(?a) <=> sub(0, ?a)
  rule abs(?a) <=> max(?a, neg(?a))
  rule sign(?a) <=> ite(gt(?a, 0), 1, ite(lt(?a, 0), neg(1), 0))
  rule divExact(?a, ?b) <=> div(?a, ?b)

  -- WI-882 DELETED four denial constraints that stood here, for two different reasons.
  --
  -- THREE WERE DUPLICATES. `div_nonzero_primary`, `div_nonzero` and `rem_nonzero` each
  -- restated `neq(?b, 0)` — the precondition `div` / `divExact` / `rem` ALREADY declare
  -- above as a live guarded `Error[DivisionByZero]` effect row. The effect row fires
  -- (a caller that neither declares nor handles it is refused at load); a plain denial
  -- does not (§8.4 — it is stored as reflected structure and never registered as a
  -- guard). Stating one precondition twice, once in a channel that works and once in
  -- one that does not, is how a reader is taught to write the dead one.
  --
  -- ONE WAS FALSE. `constraint mod_positive: gt(?b, 0) :- mod(?_, ?b)` forbade EVERY
  -- negative divisor, which is not redundant with the effect row (that forbids only
  -- ZERO) — it is a stronger claim, and a wrong one. `Int64.mod` is EUCLIDEAN, and
  -- Euclidean mod is well defined for a negative divisor: both engines compute it and
  -- since WI-875 they agree, including on the `-1` arm that would otherwise trap at
  -- `i64::MIN`. MEASURED: `mod(5, -3)` is 2, in the evaluator and in the resolver.
  -- So `b < 0` is legal and the constraint was contradicted by the implementation it
  -- claimed to constrain; `Division.mod_nonneg`, which `division.anthill` wrote to
  -- agree with this row deliberately, went with it. `mod`'s real precondition is
  -- `b ≠ 0`, and the effect row already says so.
  --
  -- AND IT COULD NOT HAVE BEEN MOVED TO A LIVE CHANNEL HERE EVEN IF IT WERE TRUE.
  -- MEASURED by building the move: restating it as `requires gt(b, 0)` on this
  -- operation is refused at load with `'anthill.prelude.Int64' overrides
  -- 'anthill.prelude.EuclideanDomain.mod' but does not refine it: it strengthens the
  -- precondition`. `Int64.mod` OVERRIDES the spec operation, an override may only
  -- WEAKEN a precondition, and `EuclideanDomain.mod` declares none — so a carrier
  -- cannot add one. The denial channel accepted the row only because it was inert:
  -- being unenforced is also being unchecked, and a claim no live channel will admit
  -- survives indefinitely in the one channel that asks nothing of it.
  -- Pinned by `wi882_deleted_denials_test`, whose header records the measurement.

  -- Bounded well-founded induction over a half-open interval [lo, hi).
  -- Proposal 025 §Auto-generated induction rules. Used by the Z3
  -- induction tactic (WI-101) to discharge ∀k. lo ≤ k < hi → P(k)
  -- obligations — the lf1 reachability lift's intended driver.
  rule induction(?P, ?lo, ?hi)
    :- ?P(?lo),
       (forall(?n),
          gte(?n, ?lo), lt(?n, ?hi), ?P(?n)
          -: ?P(add(?n, 1)))
end
